What would it take to allow theming to take place if the user was just required to enter the companies website address into an input field and hit and "apply branding" button? It would 'scrape' the website for the logo, font, colours and industry and provide a suitable cover image from a free image resource site etc?

To do “enter website → Apply branding → auto theme”, you’d be building a branding ingestion pipeline that outputs the same theme object you already use (colors/fonts/assets/charts) and then applies it via your existing token system (ThemeManager + CSS variables). The big requirement is: you’ll need a backend (or a serverless function). Pure front-end scraping will fail for most sites due to CORS and blocked asset fetching.
What you’d need (high level)
1) A backend “Branding Service” (required)
A small API that accepts a URL and returns a suggested theme payload:
{  "brand": { "name": "...", "industry": "...", "site": "..." },  "colors": { "primary": "...", "secondary": "...", "tableHeaderBg": "...", "heatmapHighlight": "..." },  "fonts": { "body": "...", "headings": "...", "googleFontsUrl": "..." },  "assets": { "logoUrl": "...", "logoDataUrl": "...", "coverImageUrl": "...", "dividerImageUrl": "..." },  "charts": { "seriesPrimary": "...", "seriesSecondary": "..." },  "confidence": { "colors": 0.7, "logo": 0.9, "fonts": 0.4, "industry": 0.6 }}
Why backend:
Fetching https://client.com HTML/CSS/images from the browser is usually blocked by CORS.
The backend can fetch pages/assets freely and normalize them (resize logo, convert to PNG/data-url, etc.).
2) “Scraping” logic (practical heuristics, not perfect)
Logo extraction (typical sources, in order):
<meta property="og:image">
<link rel="icon">, <link rel="apple-touch-icon"> (often small; fallback only)
<img> elements near header/nav with “logo” in alt/src/class/id
<svg> in header (harder: may need rasterization)
Font detection:
Parse CSS for font-family on body, headings.
Detect common patterns: Google Fonts <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/..."> or @import.
Reality check: many sites use paid/self-hosted fonts. You may need a policy:
if Google Fonts → return googleFontsUrl and font names
else map to closest safe fallback (system font stack) or ask user to choose
Color palette extraction:
Best-case: site defines CSS variables like --primary, --brand, etc. Grab them.
Otherwise: parse CSS rules for high-frequency colors, remove neutrals, cluster colors, pick dominant accents.
Then derive:
primary, secondary
tableHeaderBg/Text (ensure contrast)
heatmapHighlight (often a bright accent)
chart colors (use primary + a neutral/dark)
Industry detection:
Use structured data (application/ld+json: Organization, WebSite, sameAs)
Meta tags, page text keywords
Optionally an LLM classifier (“Given homepage text, choose industry label”)—but that’s extra cost + privacy considerations.
3) Cover/divider images from a free resource site
You’d integrate a provider API (examples):
Unsplash (API, attribution requirements depending on use)
Pexels / Pixabay (different license terms)
Process:
Use detected industry + brand keywords to search
Pick 1–3 candidates
Download + cache + optionally apply a color overlay or gradient to match brand
Return URLs (or data URLs) for your tokens --slide-cover-image / --slide-divider-image
Important: you must handle license compliance (attribution, allowed use in commercial reports, etc.).
4) UI/UX in your app
On index.html:
Add a Website URL field + Apply branding button
On click:
call backend /api/branding?url=...
show a preview (logo + palette + suggested fonts + sample slide preview)
allow manual override (color pickers + replace logo/image buttons)
Save the result into reportData.theme = 'custom' + reportData.customTheme = <payload>
5) Hook into your existing theming system
Your current pipeline already supports this nicely:
ThemeManager applies CSS variables and provides logoPath to slides.
You’d extend it so if reportData.customTheme exists, it uses that instead of ThemeRegistry.getTheme(...).
Risks / constraints (so expectations are set)
“Scrape the font” is unreliable: many sites use proprietary fonts; you can detect the name but not legally or technically embed it. A safe approach is “detect + map to fallback”.
Logos: og:image may be a hero banner, not a logo; header heuristics can fail.
Colors: sites use many colors; you need good filtering to avoid picking neutrals.
CORS / bot blocking: some sites block scraping (Cloudflare, etc.). You need retries and fallbacks.
Legal: downloading logos and photos has licensing implications; you’ll need terms/consent.
What an MVP could look like (fastest useful version)
Backend: fetch homepage HTML + CSS, extract og:image as logo fallback, detect CSS variables for colors, detect Google Fonts if present.
Cover image: use industry keywords to pull 1 candidate from a single provider (e.g., Unsplash).
UI: “Apply branding” populates a “Custom” theme and shows a preview + manual tweaks.
